167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 110 0 R
/T (The True Label \(y\))
/E (The True Label \(y\))
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E (y is the true class label for each example. In binary classification, y equals 1 if the example belongs to the positive class, and 0 if it belongs to the negative class. The formula uses y and \(1 - y\) as switches: when y = 1, only the first term log\(p\) is active; when y = 0, only the second term log\(1 - p\) is active. This elegantly handles both classes in a single expression.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 110 0 R
/T (Predicted Probability \(p\))
/E (Predicted Probability \(p\))
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 110 0 R
/E (p is the model's predicted probability that an example belongs to the positive class \(0 < p < 1\). The formula penalizes predictions using the logarithm: -log\(p\) is small when p is close to 1 \(confident and correct\), and very large when p is close to 0 \(confident and wrong\). The term log\(1 - p\) mirrors this for the negative class, penalizing high p when the true label is 0.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
